.\" karvi-command(1): the manual page of karvi's command word. Written by hand except
.\" the regions between the markers, which tools/mangen rewrites from the
.\" word's help text (make generate).
.TH KARVI-COMMAND 1 "" "karvi"
.\" Paths and options are not hyphenated, and lines are not stretched.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
karvi\-command \- run commands on one device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED SYNOPSIS: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
\fBkarvi\fR \fBcommand\fR [options] DEVICE <device command words...>
.br
\fBkarvi\fR \fBcommand\fR [options] \-\-target DEVICE \-\-cmd TEXT [\-\-cmd TEXT...]
.br
\fBkarvi\fR \fBcmd\fR [options] DEVICE \-\-cf PATH|\-
.\" END GENERATED SYNOPSIS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" BEGIN GENERATED DESCRIPTION: tools/mangen from internal/cli; edit there
.SS "Target inputs (command\-line order is kept; command connects to the first device of the assembled set)"
.TP
\fIDEVICE\fR
Positional device: the first positional word,
unless a target input precedes it
.TP
\fB\-\-target\fR \fIDEVICE\fR, \fB\-\-host\fR, \fB\-\-t\fR
Repeatable inventory or direct target, or glob
.TP
\fB\-\-tl\fR \fILIST\fR
Targets in one argument, separated by commas
or whitespace (quote a whitespace list); each
is a \-\-target at this position
.TP
\fB\-\-tf\fR \fIFILE|PATH|\-\fR
Target file, or the files directly in a
folder; \- reads stdin
.TP
\fB\-\-tfr\fR \fIPATH\fR
Target file, or a folder and its subfolders
(targets.recursion\-max\-depth, default 3)
.TP
\fB\-\-site\fR \fIGLOB\fR, \fB\-\-device\-group\fR \fIGLOB\fR, \fB\-\-select\-platform\fR \fIGLOB\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
Inventory selectors, matches at this position
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Remove name matches after duplicate removal
.PP
A GLOB matches the whole value without regard to case: * ? [a\-c] [!a\-c],
and \e escapes the next character; ^ and $ are refused. A selector value
beginning with ! removes the devices it matches and selects none; in a
target file, lines beginning with # or ! are comments.
A \-\-select\-platform value is a selector over the known platforms (the
built\-ins and the configured [platform.NAME] tables) and matches the
platform set on a device's row or source default, before any \-\-platform
acts; a value reaching no known platform is refused. A device whose platform
is not set, or falls back under platform\-resolution.on\-unknown = "warn", is
matched by no \-\-select\-platform value (it stays reachable by name, site,
group, and \-\-all).
.TP
\fB\-\-order\fR \fIdefault|sorted|shuffle|random\fR
Dispatch order for this invocation (sets the
lock\-aware dispatch.order; the shuffle key
comes from dispatch.shuffle\-key)
.PP
Folder reads skip names beginning with . readme or disabled, and editor
backups (~ .bak .swp .orig .rej #name#); an empty source follows
targets.empty\-source (error or warn).
.SS "Device commands (one form per invocation)"
.PP
Positional words after the device are joined with one space into one command.
.TP
\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fITEXT\fR, \fB\-\-command\fR, \fB\-\-c\fR
Separate command; repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-cf\fR \fIPATH|\-\fR
One command per line, sent as written; at most
once; \- reads standard input
.PP
A command ending in \er sequences is sent without them, followed by that
many carriage returns before the prompt is read (a [confirm] answer), and is
blind; nothing else in the text is interpreted.
.SS "Interactive prompts (each attaches to the \-\-cmd it follows, or to the one freeform command; a command takes blind returns or \-\-expect, not both)"
.TP
\fB\-\-expect\fR \fIPATTERN=RESPONSE\fR
When a line matching PATTERN (RE2) appears,
type RESPONSE and a return; consumed once, in
declared order; empty RESPONSE is a bare
return; at most 20 per command. A response is
device text, recorded in clear: not for a
password
.TP
\fB\-\-blind\fR
The prompt may not return: await the blind
wait in place of the command timeout, and a
prompt never seen is still a success with the
notice prompt_not_observed_after_blind_send
.TP
\fB\-\-blind\-return\fR \fIN\fR
Send N carriage returns with the command
(0..20), before any read, and treat it as
\-\-blind
.TP
\fB\-\-blind\-wait\fR \fIDURATION\fR
Prompt\-return wait for a blind command, in
place of the command timeout (0 does not wait;
sets the lock\-aware execution.blind\-wait)
.TP
\fB\-\-literal\fR
Send every command as written; no \er
interpretation; a RESPONSE is always as written
.SS "Options"
.TP
\fB\-\-platform\fR \fINAME\fR
The platform the devices of this invocation run
as, whatever their inventory rows say: a
built\-in or a configured [platform.NAME]
table; a name, never a pattern. login and
command apply it to their one device, run to
every device of the set (a direct target in no
inventory runs as generic without it).
Without it an inventory row's platform
applies, then the source default, then
platform\-resolution.default (else generic); an
unknown row platform is refused unless
platform\-resolution.on\-unknown = "warn"
.TP
\fB\-\-pi\fR, \fB\-\-pn\fR, \fB\-\-pr\fR, \fB\-\-pj\fR, \fB\-\-pa\fR, \fB\-\-pg\fR
Short for \-\-platform cisco_iosxe, cisco_nxos,
cisco_iosxr, juniper_junos, arista_eos, generic
.TP
\fB\-\-management\-address\fR \fIIP\fR, \fB\-\-address\fR, \fB\-\-a\fR
Literal management IP; does not select the target
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIN\fR
Device service port
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\fR \fISELECTOR\fR
default, system, native, preferred, telnet,
or a configured [ssh.transports] slot
.TP
\fB\-\-ssh\-host\-key\-policy\fR \fIaccept\-new|secure|insecure\fR
The host\-key policy (ssh.host\-key\-policy):
accept\-new accepts and persists a new key and
rejects a changed one (the default); secure
requires a matching pre\-enrolled key before
access; insecure accepts unknown or changed
keys with prominent warnings
.TP
\fB\-\-ssh\-known\-hosts\-file\fR \fIPATH|auto\fR
Unified karvi trust store
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fItext|jsonl|json\fR
Text (default), compact JSON Lines, or an
indented JSON array for human inspection
.TP
\fB\-\-echo\fR
Show prompt plus sent command in text output,
including device\-reported command errors
.TP
\fB\-\-border\fR
Replace the configured border with dashes
.TP
\fB\-\-noborder\fR
Disable configured and dynamic borders
.TP
\fB\-\-continue\-device\-on\-error\fR
Do not stop later commands after a device error
.TP
\fB\-\-nof\fR
No output files: run and display, create no job
folder (output.persist\-command=false)
.TP
\fB\-\-of[=PATH]\fR
Output files on (output.persist\-command=true),
to the configured folder or to PATH
(output.root); PATH only with =, as the next
word is the device (a path there is refused).
The opposite of \-\-nof
.TP
\fB\-\-cd=PATH\fR
Also write the device's output to PATH/NAME,
one file named by the device, a "! COMMAND"
marker before each command's output and
nothing else, replaced only when the device
succeeds (crun.directory; ~ expanded, relative
to the working directory; auto the collection
tree)
.TP
\fB\-\-fs=SUFFIX\fR
Append SUFFIX to the collection file's name
(\-\-fs=.cfg writes NAME.cfg); without \-\-cd it
is \-\-cd=. as well
.TP
\fB\-\-address\-authority\fR \fIclient|daemon\fR
Who selects the address: the client at planning
(default) or the daemon at prepare
.TP
\fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress configured header, footer, and border
.TP
\fB\-\-debug\fR
Safe transport, resolution, and timing diagnostics
.TP
\fB\-\-ping\fR
Send two ICMP probes to each target before its
transport; skip a target that answers neither
.TP
\fB\-\-noping\fR
Send no ICMP probes (the default unless
network.ping\-targets is true)
.TP
\fB\-\-ipv4\fR, \fB\-\-4\fR
Prefer IPv4 when both families are available
.TP
\fB\-\-ipv6\fR, \fB\-\-6\fR
Prefer IPv6 when both families are available
.TP
\fB\-\-help\fR, \fB\-\-h\fR
Show this help
.PP
Options are recognized before the device and between the device and the first
device command word. Once device text begins, karvi recognizes no options,
abbreviations, \-\-, or help flags: "command r1 show clock \-\-echo" sends
"show clock \-\-echo". All commands use one authenticated SSH session and
execute sequentially; OpenSSH connection reuse is disabled.
.\" END GENERATED DESCRIPTION
.SH OUTPUT
.B command
runs in the client process, never through the daemon, on the first device
of its target set: one session, the commands in order.
In text the device's output follows its header line, with the prompt and
the command before each block under
.BR \-\-echo ,
and the display ends with the footer
.RB ( display.run.footer ,
.BR "! exit=EXIT elapsed=TIME artifacts=DIR" ).
A job folder is kept as for a run, its files described in
.BR karvi\-run (1);
.B \-\-nof
keeps none, and
.BI \-\-cd= PATH
writes the device's collection file as
.BR karvi\-crun (1)
describes, unfiltered.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B karvi command r1 show version
One command, the words after the device joined.
.TP
.B karvi cmd r1 \-\-cmd "show clock" \-\-cmd "show ip interface brief"
Two commands in one session.
.TP
.B karvi cmd r1 \-\-cmd "copy running\-config startup\-config" \-\-expect "Destination filename="
A prompt answered with a bare return.
.TP
.B karvi command \-\-nof r1 show clock
No job folder.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR karvi (1),
.BR karvi\-run (1),
.BR karvi\-login (1)
